WEBVTT

00:13.056 --> 00:14.623
MAN: Are you in San Diego?

00:14.666 --> 00:15.798
BILLY EVANS:
We are in San Diego.

00:15.841 --> 00:18.235
We are at our, uh...

00:18.279 --> 00:20.629
at the little rental
that we have down here,

00:20.672 --> 00:22.065
which has been a really nice,

00:22.109 --> 00:24.111
like, port in the storm.

00:28.941 --> 00:31.335
MAN: And then you mentioned

00:31.379 --> 00:36.471
you have someone who's gonna
start telling Elizabeth's--

00:36.514 --> 00:39.648
Yeah. So we've
started documenting, like,

00:39.691 --> 00:42.216
Liz's life right now.

00:42.259 --> 00:45.436
You know, we have, like,

00:45.480 --> 00:47.482
people kind of
constantly around us,

00:47.525 --> 00:49.005
starting to capture
these moments,

00:49.049 --> 00:51.225
because what we're
going through right now--

00:51.964 --> 00:53.792
Well, I mean
at its simplest level,

00:53.836 --> 00:55.707
I feel like the reason why

00:55.751 --> 00:57.274
Liz has been turned
into this monster is

00:57.318 --> 00:59.885
because nobody knows
who she is. Right?

00:59.929 --> 01:01.626
And, like, in absence
of knowing somebody,

01:01.670 --> 01:05.413
it's really easy to make them
feel other and scary.

01:05.456 --> 01:06.892
Like, it's easy
to build up this myth

01:06.936 --> 01:08.024
around who the person is

01:08.068 --> 01:09.895
when you don't know
who they are.

01:09.939 --> 01:11.680
So we've kind of
made the decision

01:11.723 --> 01:14.857
that the best way for people
to understand who she is,

01:14.900 --> 01:18.208
is by just giving, like,
radical transparency

01:18.252 --> 01:20.645
and, you know,
as much access as possible

01:20.689 --> 01:22.691
into our daily lives.

01:22.734 --> 01:25.172
Everything from, like,
you know,

01:25.215 --> 01:27.783
putting the kids to sleep to

01:28.566 --> 01:32.309
you know, what we're building
and everything in between.

01:32.353 --> 01:33.745
[ELIZABETH HOLMES
SHUSHING SOFTLY]

01:33.789 --> 01:36.226
So one thing at a time.

01:36.270 --> 01:38.272
But what I was saying is,

01:38.315 --> 01:41.144
it would be so great if there
was a way in which you could--

01:41.188 --> 01:43.494
[CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY]

02:01.599 --> 02:03.732
[ELIZABETH YELLING EXCITEDLY]

02:06.778 --> 02:08.171
ELIZABETH:
Do you wanna run in?

02:08.215 --> 02:11.957
Should we go... in?

02:21.750 --> 02:25.710
On the low end is...

02:25.754 --> 02:27.147
-Okay.
-...120 nanometers.

02:27.538 --> 02:29.540
Um, and--

02:29.584 --> 02:31.542
But, we don't even--
You know, it's--

02:31.890 --> 02:33.588
These aren't relevant
for human systems.

02:33.631 --> 02:34.719
[BOY SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY]

02:34.763 --> 02:35.981
Like, some of them are like,
you know,

02:36.025 --> 02:37.983
infections
of frog embryos and stuff.

02:38.027 --> 02:39.985
-Yeah, yeah.
-So, like, you know I think--

02:40.029 --> 02:41.987
Yeah. I just wanna show
that it works.

02:42.031 --> 02:43.380
The magic show!

02:43.859 --> 02:46.209
So we know how it works.

02:46.253 --> 02:47.558
And, my love,

02:47.602 --> 02:49.038
you said a small molecule

02:49.081 --> 02:50.213
is what you've seen
so far is--

02:50.257 --> 02:51.562
Way too small.
Way, way, way too small.

02:51.606 --> 02:53.390
Is what, though?
What are the--

02:53.825 --> 02:56.219
[BILLY SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

03:06.664 --> 03:09.232
REPORTER: Barefoot on the
beach with a baby in her arms.

03:09.276 --> 03:11.626
There have been several
Elizabeth Holmes sightings

03:11.669 --> 03:13.236
right here in Del Mar,

03:13.280 --> 03:16.674
as she continues to postpone
her time behind bars.

03:16.718 --> 03:19.286
I did testify
in my criminal trial

03:19.329 --> 03:23.899
and I was acquitted of
the counts associated with

03:24.813 --> 03:27.990
the charges on patients
and patient testing.

03:28.033 --> 03:30.993
And I don't have any money.

03:31.036 --> 03:32.908
I'm deeply in debt

03:34.126 --> 03:37.608
for the legal fees associated
with my criminal case.

03:37.652 --> 03:40.916
But I do want the ability
to defend myself

03:40.959 --> 03:43.397
and, um, and would ask

03:43.440 --> 03:46.008
for your consideration
in that.

03:50.969 --> 03:52.623
Thank you, Your Honor.

03:56.323 --> 04:01.502
Our work is in being able to
make testing more accessible.

04:01.545 --> 04:03.417
REPORTER: Elizabeth
Holmes built her company,

04:03.460 --> 04:05.027
Theranos, on this invention

04:05.070 --> 04:07.247
she called
"the iPod of healthcare",

04:07.290 --> 04:09.249
a miniaturized blood analyzer

04:09.292 --> 04:11.512
that would disrupt
the $60 billion

04:11.555 --> 04:14.558
lab-testing industry.
It was, she claimed, quote...

04:14.602 --> 04:17.474
"the most important thing
humanity has ever built".

04:17.518 --> 04:19.084
And Elizabeth Holmes became

04:19.128 --> 04:21.565
the youngest self-made
female billionaire

04:21.609 --> 04:23.219
in the world.

04:23.263 --> 04:26.831
She was also at the center of
a massive multi-year fraud.

04:26.875 --> 04:29.704
"Each day I greet the sun"

04:30.182 --> 04:32.750
"and the earth, the trees."

04:32.794 --> 04:35.449
She says,
"I train my body for battle,"

04:35.492 --> 04:37.364
"and I train my mind, too."

04:37.407 --> 04:39.801
PODCAST NARRATOR:
From ABC Audio, this is

04:39.844 --> 04:42.369
The Dropout:
Elizabeth Holmes on Trial.

04:42.412 --> 04:44.588
INTERVIEWER: You said that
Ms. Holmes was manipulative.

04:44.632 --> 04:46.068
What did you mean by that?

04:46.111 --> 04:48.026
MAN: You had these
older men around her

04:48.070 --> 04:49.419
that she manipulated.

04:49.463 --> 04:50.942
WOMAN 1: She just led
these men on.

04:50.986 --> 04:52.640
And she had a charm.

04:52.683 --> 04:54.381
MAN 2: I think she's
really good at telling you

04:54.424 --> 04:57.340
what you need
to hear to keep going.

04:57.384 --> 04:59.255
WOMAN 2:
She's a pathological liar.

04:59.299 --> 05:00.604
MAN 4: She told everyone
the tech worked

05:00.648 --> 05:02.084
and it didn't work at all.

05:02.127 --> 05:03.477
MALE REPORTER 1: Theranos
founder Elizabeth Holmes

05:03.520 --> 05:05.870
is set to begin her prison
sentence on May 30th.

05:05.914 --> 05:08.046
A federal court rejected her
request to stay out on bail

05:08.090 --> 05:09.483
while appealing her conviction

05:09.526 --> 05:11.572
on fraud
and conspiracy charges.

05:11.615 --> 05:12.964
FEMALE REPORTER 1:
Sunny Balwani was indicted,

05:13.008 --> 05:15.576
along with his ex-girlfriend,
Elizabeth Holmes.

05:15.619 --> 05:18.405
They really run this company
as a couple

05:18.448 --> 05:20.885
and they perpetrate this fraud
as a couple.

05:20.929 --> 05:22.844
MALE REPORTER 2: Prosecutors
say he and Elizabeth Holmes

05:22.887 --> 05:25.150
were partners
in virtually everything.

05:25.194 --> 05:26.326
FEMALE REPORTER 2:
Where she claimed that

05:26.369 --> 05:27.805
they could use
one drop of blood

05:27.849 --> 05:29.503
to test a bunch
of different things,

05:29.546 --> 05:32.549
but it turned out that
that technology did not work.

05:32.593 --> 05:34.856
She was convicted
on four counts of fraud

05:34.899 --> 05:36.727
for defrauding investors.

05:36.771 --> 05:41.253
But she was not found guilty
of defrauding patients.

05:46.737 --> 05:48.086
BILLY:
"Miss Holmes has not spoken

05:48.130 --> 05:49.653
"to the media since 2016."

05:49.697 --> 05:51.481
"Her trajectory launched
a cottage industry"

05:51.525 --> 05:54.528
"of podcasts, TV shows,
Halloween costumes,"

05:54.571 --> 05:55.964
"and the actress
Amanda Seyfried's

05:56.007 --> 05:58.923
"Emmy Award-winning turn

05:58.967 --> 06:02.840
"as Miss Holmes
in the Hulu's The Dropout."

06:02.884 --> 06:05.539
"The black turtlenecks,
the Kabuki red lipstick,"

06:05.582 --> 06:08.280
"the green juices,
the dancing to Lil Wayne,"

06:08.324 --> 06:10.065
"somewhere along the way,
Ms. Holmes says,

06:10.108 --> 06:13.460
"the person, whoever that is,
got lost."

06:13.503 --> 06:15.113
"At one point,
I tell her that I heard

06:15.157 --> 06:17.072
"Jennifer Lawrence
had pulled out of

06:17.115 --> 06:18.465
"portraying her in a movie.

06:18.508 --> 06:20.075
"She replied,
almost reflectively,"

06:20.118 --> 06:21.337
"'They're not playing me.

06:21.381 --> 06:24.819
"They're playing a character
I created.'"

06:25.733 --> 06:27.561
-That seems like another...
-I did not say that.

06:27.604 --> 06:29.606
...thing that you would never
say in a million years.

06:29.650 --> 06:31.173
You would never say,
"a character I created."

06:31.216 --> 06:32.304
You probably said,

06:32.348 --> 06:33.828
"a character
that they created."

06:33.871 --> 06:36.700
Mm-hm. We should probably
just, like, send them an email

06:36.744 --> 06:38.702
about stuff that I didn't say,
just so that--

06:38.746 --> 06:41.009
Yeah. I mean, we have
recordings of a lot of it.

06:41.052 --> 06:44.665
Like, 90% of the stuff that's
quoted in here, we recorded.

06:46.928 --> 06:48.233
NATHAN FIELDER: Do you do that
with me?

06:48.277 --> 06:49.409
BILLY: No.

06:50.148 --> 06:52.716
Not yet.
It's probably a good idea.

06:54.196 --> 06:55.589
The way in which
they use words--

06:55.632 --> 06:56.938
We should probably
talk to Gwen and Risa

06:56.981 --> 06:58.809
just about, like,
whether we--

06:58.853 --> 07:01.333
I mean, we need to go back
on a few of these things

07:01.377 --> 07:03.640
because it's just, like,
factually inaccurate.

07:39.284 --> 07:40.677
-ELIZABETH: I mean, just...
-[BABY CRIES]

07:40.721 --> 07:42.940
-Hey! It's okay. You can come.
-BILLY: C'mon. C'mon.

07:42.984 --> 07:44.986
-You're coming with us.
-Yeah.

07:45.029 --> 07:46.204
Your voice has been heard.

07:46.248 --> 07:47.684
-Yeah.
-The council has spoken.

07:47.728 --> 07:49.077
-Yeah.
-Want some more orange?

07:49.120 --> 07:51.253
-Wills can always come.
-Yeah?

07:51.296 --> 07:52.472
-Hi, guys.
-BOY: More apple.

07:52.515 --> 07:53.908
BILLY: More apple.

07:53.951 --> 07:55.126
ELIZABETH: More apple.

07:55.649 --> 07:57.694
-Do you want more bagel?
-I'm all right.

07:57.738 --> 07:59.391
-Thank you so much.
-Blood orange?

07:59.435 --> 08:02.394
The world believes a reality
about me

08:03.178 --> 08:07.922
that isn't true.
And so what is the reality?

08:07.965 --> 08:09.358
And how did we get here

08:09.401 --> 08:10.881
where all these people believe
these things

08:10.925 --> 08:12.535
that didn't happen?

08:12.579 --> 08:14.015
How did that happen?

08:15.407 --> 08:18.323
But, you didn't commit
any crime, you think?

08:18.367 --> 08:21.152
I know I didn't commit
any crime.

08:25.069 --> 08:28.203
Can I tell you a little bit of
an insecurity of mine?

08:28.725 --> 08:31.511
-Always.
-Okay.

08:32.555 --> 08:37.560
I read that you asked, um,
General Mattis

08:37.865 --> 08:39.867
to be on the board
of your company.

08:39.910 --> 08:41.564
-Hm.
-And, you know,

08:41.608 --> 08:43.523
he's a military guy,

08:43.566 --> 08:45.307
but he's not a science guy.

08:46.090 --> 08:48.136
-Hm.
-So part of me maybe

08:48.179 --> 08:49.964
is a little insecure that

08:50.660 --> 08:53.663
you look at me as someone who

08:54.664 --> 08:58.363
is uneducated on science

08:58.973 --> 09:02.063
and scientific things
so I can't question you

09:02.106 --> 09:07.111
in a way that another person,
who is, uh, would.

09:07.416 --> 09:12.116
So why, uh, why me?

09:12.160 --> 09:15.032
I think we've been thinking
about this,

09:15.076 --> 09:16.991
first and foremost,
as a human story.

09:17.034 --> 09:19.559
'Cause you could've
gone to science reporters

09:19.602 --> 09:20.995
and investigative--

09:21.038 --> 09:23.867
-Sure.
-But you didn't, because what?

09:23.911 --> 09:26.348
Do they suck or do you think
they suck, or something?

09:26.391 --> 09:29.003
-I don't, actually, at all.
-Oh, okay. I don't know.

09:29.046 --> 09:31.005
I mean, the purpose,
at least to me,

09:31.048 --> 09:33.181
the purpose of this
was not to,

09:33.224 --> 09:34.965
um...

09:35.009 --> 09:37.402
to litigate the science,

09:37.446 --> 09:41.015
although I really hope
people look at

09:41.058 --> 09:43.408
the testimony in the case

09:43.452 --> 09:45.585
and all the documents
that support that.

09:46.455 --> 09:50.938
It starts with who I am

09:50.981 --> 09:55.507
and what my intent was
and still is.

09:58.728 --> 10:02.471
I do wanna say to you
for just full transparency,

10:02.514 --> 10:07.607
I have read a lot
about Theranos and you

10:07.650 --> 10:11.828
and, you know, I do believe
there is a lot of evidence

10:11.872 --> 10:13.874
that points to the fact that

10:13.917 --> 10:17.704
you defrauded investors

10:17.747 --> 10:22.796
and also deceived,
you know, regulators

10:22.839 --> 10:26.103
and the public and patients

10:26.147 --> 10:28.018
and, um,

10:29.629 --> 10:31.456
were involved
in conspiracy, too.

10:31.848 --> 10:33.067
I-- Look.

10:33.110 --> 10:35.547
I mean, I understand

10:35.591 --> 10:37.506
what people have said
about me.

10:37.549 --> 10:41.553
And I understand that people
believe those things.

10:44.295 --> 10:47.559
All I know is my truth. And...

10:53.000 --> 10:55.480
my love for my family and that

10:57.961 --> 11:00.398
I could be taken away
from them.

11:08.885 --> 11:10.974
[SOFTLY] I can't, sorry.

11:18.373 --> 11:20.244
That must be hard.

11:35.825 --> 11:37.131
Do you want, like,
a tissue or something?

11:37.174 --> 11:38.567
-[CHUCKLES] Yeah.
-Hold on. You stay there.

11:38.610 --> 11:40.438
-I need one. [SNIFFLES]
-Let me get it for you.

11:43.093 --> 11:44.921
NATHAN: Um...

11:47.010 --> 11:48.011
Here ya go.

11:48.838 --> 11:49.883
Thank you. [CHUCKLES]

11:49.926 --> 11:51.232
Okay.

11:54.626 --> 11:55.845
[SNIFFLES]

12:01.982 --> 12:03.853
BILLY: [SOFTLY] My baby.

12:06.943 --> 12:08.249
Okay.

12:08.292 --> 12:10.251
I think we're gonna need
to put a pin in that.

12:10.294 --> 12:11.731
Okay.

12:11.774 --> 12:13.080
'Cause it's 12:20 now,
and, like,

12:14.734 --> 12:17.562
you have a ton of calls
that you need to do.

12:17.606 --> 12:20.348
I don't wanna speak for you,
but, I'll let ya--

12:23.917 --> 12:25.440
Whatever you want.

12:32.969 --> 12:34.971
NATHAN: So what do you think
you guys wanna film next?

12:35.667 --> 12:38.845
Um, well I was planning
to go work out,

12:38.888 --> 12:40.281
if you'd like to come

12:40.324 --> 12:42.283
-with me to that.
-Oh. Thanks. Um...

12:43.545 --> 12:45.242
Pick your piece wisely.

12:45.677 --> 12:47.767
-What is this, again?
-It's a pink pineapple.

12:48.158 --> 12:50.944
In talking to Liz,
it sounded like

12:50.987 --> 12:54.817
the vision she had
with Theranos hasn't died.

12:54.861 --> 12:57.298
Is this just kind of like
a random thought,

12:57.341 --> 12:59.779
or are there plans for this?

13:00.649 --> 13:03.652
No there's not just plans,

13:03.695 --> 13:05.393
but there's plans
in the works.

13:05.436 --> 13:07.830
You know, like,

13:07.874 --> 13:09.179
we've taken a lot of
the first steps

13:09.223 --> 13:10.485
to make this happen.

13:10.964 --> 13:14.054
Um, and as it relates to her

13:15.969 --> 13:17.492
intention with this,

13:19.276 --> 13:21.235
she hasn't stopped
talking about it

13:21.278 --> 13:23.846
since the moment I met her.

13:23.890 --> 13:25.239
Wow.

13:25.282 --> 13:26.849
The idea is to...

13:26.893 --> 13:29.939
to put the band back together
and do it all again.

13:30.810 --> 13:33.334
You know, like,
we're committed to the cause.

13:35.336 --> 13:38.513
So 2.0. You know, there's 1.0
which was the first version.

13:39.731 --> 13:41.124
And, um,

13:41.168 --> 13:43.561
here's the second iteration
of the same idea.

13:46.913 --> 13:49.393
-Theranos 2.0.
-Yeah.

13:52.179 --> 13:54.659
NATHAN: What is up first
for you guys tomorrow?

13:54.703 --> 13:55.922
We've got a 9:20 appointment
for Ivy.

13:55.965 --> 13:57.619
-Okay.
-And we typically don't like

13:57.662 --> 13:58.925
going on the weekends, 'cause

13:58.968 --> 14:00.187
on the weekends there's,
like, a million people.

14:00.230 --> 14:01.536
-I mean, I think--
-So, like, Friday morning--

14:01.579 --> 14:03.625
What day were you thinking
the bonfire?

14:03.668 --> 14:05.105
I haven't thought that through
yet.

14:05.148 --> 14:06.149
I was gonna call my brother

14:06.193 --> 14:07.237
and see
what his availability is.

14:07.281 --> 14:09.109
Okay.

14:09.152 --> 14:11.067
One conversation that we had
yesterday with somebody...

14:11.111 --> 14:13.548
with the guy who brought us to

14:13.591 --> 14:17.247
the studio yesterday is...

14:18.858 --> 14:20.860
that he said
that he would be very willing

14:20.903 --> 14:24.341
to speak with you on camera

14:24.385 --> 14:25.908
but obscuring his identity
to talk about--

14:25.952 --> 14:28.389
Why'd you take the eggs over?

14:28.432 --> 14:30.739
What was she doing wrong
with mixing the eggs?

14:30.782 --> 14:32.436
I just wanted to help her,

14:32.480 --> 14:34.047
so she could focus
on talking to you.

14:34.090 --> 14:35.744
-Okay.
-What I was doing wrong is

14:35.787 --> 14:37.137
he doesn't think that
I'm mixing them

14:37.180 --> 14:39.008
-vigorously enough.
-She does it like this. She,

14:39.052 --> 14:40.618
-like, does it very lightly.
-And therefore,

14:40.662 --> 14:43.360
the eggs don't taste as good
because you--

14:44.144 --> 14:46.233
Really wanna make sure
that they're well stirred.

14:46.276 --> 14:48.104
She's got it. She's got it.

14:48.148 --> 14:49.932
-Thank you.
-You're welcome.

15:08.777 --> 15:10.605
NATHAN: How did you two meet?

15:11.258 --> 15:15.566
We met at a friend's party
on October 7th of 2017.

15:15.610 --> 15:17.177
And, um...

15:20.223 --> 15:21.746
yeah, we just...

15:23.487 --> 15:25.794
Sorry, I got distracted. Um...

15:25.837 --> 15:27.056
By what?

15:27.100 --> 15:29.711
-Just a thought. Um...
-What's the thought?

15:29.754 --> 15:32.453
I was trying to think back
to that moment,

15:32.496 --> 15:34.107
and I was just...
I don't know.

15:34.150 --> 15:35.151
There's so many things that

15:35.195 --> 15:36.370
went through my head
in that moment.

15:36.413 --> 15:37.762
-Like, when you met her?
-Yeah.

15:37.806 --> 15:39.025
Like what?

15:41.288 --> 15:43.943
Well, first,
I didn't know who she was.

15:43.986 --> 15:47.076
But did you know who she was
in general?

15:47.120 --> 15:48.425
Like, did you know
who Elizabeth Holmes

15:48.469 --> 15:49.687
was at that time?

15:49.731 --> 15:50.732
I did, yeah. I mean,

15:50.775 --> 15:52.081
I was working
in Silicon Valley,

15:52.125 --> 15:53.822
and I had just toured
her building

15:53.865 --> 15:55.215
-the week before.
-Uh-huh.

15:55.258 --> 15:56.868
Because we were looking at
renting her building.

15:56.912 --> 15:58.696
So, like, I knew exactly
who she was.

15:58.740 --> 16:00.002
I'd read a lot
of the same media

16:00.046 --> 16:01.308
that everybody else had.

16:01.351 --> 16:03.397
So how'd you not
recognize her?

16:03.440 --> 16:06.095
She was, like, wearing
a big hat and sunglasses,

16:06.139 --> 16:08.228
and she had, like,
a band aid on her face.

16:08.271 --> 16:11.318
-So I didn't know who she was.
-Why a band aid?

16:11.361 --> 16:14.843
I had just gotten a husky,
and he'd scratched my face.

16:15.539 --> 16:16.714
He was a puppy.

16:16.758 --> 16:18.934
And I had this cut on my face,

16:18.978 --> 16:21.589
so that may have also
been part of the idea

16:21.632 --> 16:23.417
with the sunglasses.

16:23.460 --> 16:24.809
Was it love at first sight?

16:24.853 --> 16:25.941
I think for me it was.

16:25.985 --> 16:27.290
-Really?
-I don't-- Yeah.

16:27.334 --> 16:28.596
I was, like, very--

16:28.639 --> 16:29.814
I was trying
to be flirtatious.

16:29.858 --> 16:31.425
-You were?
-Yeah, definitely.

16:31.468 --> 16:33.688
What are you like
when you flirt?

16:33.731 --> 16:35.995
I just-- I didn't know
what to say to him,

16:36.038 --> 16:37.866
so I was just kinda
staring at him.

16:37.909 --> 16:40.260
So is that your technique,
you, like, hold eye contact?

16:40.303 --> 16:41.348
Yeah.

16:41.391 --> 16:42.523
Yeah?

16:43.915 --> 16:47.136
And her and I started talking
and very quickly,

16:47.180 --> 16:48.529
kind of like, everything else
ceased to exist.

16:48.572 --> 16:49.921
Like, it was just
the two of us.

16:49.965 --> 16:51.358
You know when you're in
a conversation with somebody

16:51.401 --> 16:52.576
and there's
other people around,

16:52.620 --> 16:54.056
but it really just
becomes the two of you.

16:54.100 --> 16:55.840
I mean, at the time in 2017,

16:55.884 --> 16:57.929
there was a lot of bad news
about Theranos.

16:57.973 --> 16:59.583
It was only bad news.

16:59.627 --> 17:01.237
And you just didn't know
who she was?

17:01.281 --> 17:02.456
No idea. Yeah.

17:02.499 --> 17:04.371
When did you realize
it was her?

17:04.414 --> 17:05.763
Maybe an hour into talking.

17:05.807 --> 17:08.201
And what did she say
that made you realize?

17:08.244 --> 17:11.726
Something about giving people
access to healthcare

17:11.769 --> 17:14.946
which made me kind of realize,
oh, testing and diagnostics--

17:14.990 --> 17:17.819
Oh. That's who this is.

17:17.862 --> 17:19.125
It was super-fast.

17:19.168 --> 17:23.303
It went from zero to 100
really quickly.

17:23.694 --> 17:25.783
I mean, the first time
that he started talking about

17:25.827 --> 17:28.917
spending our life together,
I thought he was kidding.

17:28.960 --> 17:30.484
And I asked him about 30 times

17:30.527 --> 17:32.051
if he was serious,
because I was, like,

17:32.442 --> 17:34.662
are you, like--
Do you mean that?

17:34.705 --> 17:37.752
Do you feel like it would've
gone in the same direction

17:37.795 --> 17:38.883
with you guys

17:39.188 --> 17:41.843
if he knew from the very start
it was you?

17:42.539 --> 17:44.541
-As opposed to--
-I don't know.

17:44.585 --> 17:47.066
I don't know. Maybe not.
You know?

17:48.067 --> 17:51.070
Maybe not.
I mean, the bias is so strong.

17:51.809 --> 17:54.725
I fell in love
in that first conversation.

17:54.769 --> 17:57.337
Before or after the--

17:57.380 --> 17:59.774
she revealed she was
the founder of Theranos?

17:59.817 --> 18:00.862
Probably before.

18:00.905 --> 18:02.516
-Okay.
-Um, it was--

18:02.559 --> 18:04.692
So you fell in love,
like, in a few minutes.

18:04.735 --> 18:06.911
-So then here you were stuck--
-And maybe in an hour,

18:06.955 --> 18:09.044
you know? It was just there.
It was, like, clear.

18:09.088 --> 18:10.959
You couldn't even
see her face, though.

18:11.002 --> 18:13.092
Yeah, I guess that wasn't
what mattered to me.

18:13.570 --> 18:16.530
Like, you're
a very handsome man.

18:16.573 --> 18:18.140
-Thank you, Nathan.
-You're an attractive guy.

18:18.184 --> 18:21.274
I'm sure you're popular
with women,

18:21.317 --> 18:22.536
I'm guessing?

18:24.929 --> 18:26.496
Yeah, I like women.

18:26.540 --> 18:29.369
I know you like them.
But, like, I'm sure--

18:29.412 --> 18:30.674
Yeah, I was dating
other people

18:30.718 --> 18:32.415
-at the same time I met Liz.
-You were?

18:32.459 --> 18:34.678
-Yeah.
-How many?

18:35.157 --> 18:38.073
Nobody too seriously.
Maybe three people.

18:38.117 --> 18:40.031
-And then you meet Liz.
-Yeah.

18:40.075 --> 18:42.077
-And she gets indicted...
-Yeah.

18:42.121 --> 18:43.600
-...for a crime.
-Yeah.

18:43.644 --> 18:46.603
And you are choosing,
at that point

18:46.647 --> 18:48.475
to be, like, okay,

18:48.518 --> 18:50.041
this is what I want...

18:50.085 --> 18:51.391
-Yeah.
-...for my life.

18:51.434 --> 18:52.566
Yeah.

18:52.609 --> 18:54.437
Why?

18:56.178 --> 18:59.007
I think which speaks to
how much I love her.

18:59.616 --> 19:01.662
Um, yeah.

19:02.402 --> 19:03.316
That's cool.

19:03.968 --> 19:06.014
Yeah, it's amazing.

19:09.365 --> 19:12.803
I mean, weren't you worried
she was sort of,

19:12.847 --> 19:14.544
like, scamming you?

19:15.110 --> 19:17.721
100%. 100%.

19:17.765 --> 19:20.289
But what did she have
to gain from me?

19:20.333 --> 19:22.204
Well, isn't your family rich?

19:23.510 --> 19:26.252
Yeah. I think
that there's, like,

19:26.295 --> 19:27.818
a lot of different levels
of wealth, right?

19:27.862 --> 19:29.429
Okay.

19:29.472 --> 19:33.128
And, I think I certainly come
from a family that, um...

19:33.911 --> 19:36.305
has done well, but, like--

19:37.611 --> 19:41.092
The level of wealth,
you should-- you should--

19:41.136 --> 19:43.486
I'm not gonna answer
that question.

19:44.183 --> 19:45.967
More your parents.

19:46.010 --> 19:47.751
Is that where the money is,
or?

19:47.795 --> 19:49.231
I mean, they've built
a business. I've gotta go.

19:49.275 --> 19:50.624
-You-- Okay.
-It's 5:30. I--

19:50.667 --> 19:52.626
My cousin's thing started
at 5:15.

19:52.669 --> 19:54.367
Oh, okay.
Sorry. I didn't know.

19:55.846 --> 19:57.239
Um...

20:14.648 --> 20:17.564
How do you decide what to
put in and what to leave out?

20:18.260 --> 20:20.741
Right now I'm putting
everything in,

20:20.784 --> 20:23.744
and then I'll cut

20:24.223 --> 20:25.920
once I sort of see it
all together

20:25.963 --> 20:27.617
and how it fits together.

20:31.795 --> 20:34.407
Did you write
about Sunny at all?

20:34.450 --> 20:35.582
I did.

20:35.625 --> 20:36.887
It's a...

20:38.454 --> 20:40.935
a big part of my life. Mm.

20:43.154 --> 20:45.853
How long
were you guys together?

20:46.636 --> 20:48.290
Almost 12 years.

20:48.334 --> 20:50.249
-Wow.
-Yeah.

20:53.252 --> 20:55.515
What was Sunny's role
at Theranos?

20:56.211 --> 20:59.388
He was the president

20:59.432 --> 21:01.303
and Chief Operating Officer
of the company

21:01.347 --> 21:03.697
and the Vice Chairman
of the Board.

21:03.740 --> 21:05.133
And what was your role?

21:05.176 --> 21:08.267
I was the CEO
and the Chairman of the Board.

21:09.877 --> 21:15.230
Um, I-- I believed in him,
because I thought

21:16.275 --> 21:20.279
he was the best in the world
at what he was doing.

21:20.714 --> 21:22.672
But I know that

21:23.760 --> 21:28.199
I didn't question him

21:29.070 --> 21:32.291
in the way
that I otherwise would have,

21:32.334 --> 21:36.164
had that relationship
not been in place.

21:40.081 --> 21:42.692
NATHAN: Do you feel like

21:42.736 --> 21:44.868
you're an overly
trusting person?

21:44.912 --> 21:46.740
For sure.

21:47.088 --> 21:48.611
But I think
that's the best way

21:48.655 --> 21:53.660
to build the relationships
that are successful ones,

21:53.703 --> 21:55.966
because I think if you start

21:56.010 --> 21:57.490
from a place
of complete trust,

21:57.533 --> 22:00.710
then if it is gonna be
a good relationship,

22:00.754 --> 22:03.191
it will be that much stronger
because of it.

22:06.237 --> 22:08.457
NATHAN: That's a really
nice philosophy.

22:10.285 --> 22:13.332
Well, that's how Billy
and I met

22:13.375 --> 22:16.596
and built the relationship
that we have.

22:35.136 --> 22:36.267
TRAINER: Hold it.

22:36.311 --> 22:38.008
Open, open, open. One.

22:38.052 --> 22:39.880
-Two. Three. [GRUNTS]
-TRAINER: Two. Three.

22:39.923 --> 22:41.098
TRAINER: One. Two. Three.

22:41.142 --> 22:42.665
Again, that's one. C'mon.

22:42.709 --> 22:44.275
-C'mon, man!
-[GROANING]

22:44.319 --> 22:46.582
-Two. Stay.
-[BILLY GRUNTS]

23:09.344 --> 23:10.519
Sorry.

23:14.218 --> 23:16.656
NATHAN: So where did things
get lost?

23:16.699 --> 23:18.788
Like, how are you
where you are right now?

23:20.921 --> 23:23.053
I don't-- I don't know.

23:23.097 --> 23:24.185
I...

23:25.795 --> 23:26.883
What's your best guess?

23:26.927 --> 23:29.886
I mean, if you--
you feel like everything

23:29.930 --> 23:31.845
that you did was above board

23:31.888 --> 23:35.152
and you operated in good faith
and...

23:35.805 --> 23:38.939
Do you feel like
your legal team, you know,

23:38.982 --> 23:42.029
their advice has led you
to this point?

23:42.072 --> 23:43.552
No.

23:43.596 --> 23:47.208
I think they've put
their hearts and souls

23:47.251 --> 23:50.037
into fighting it. You know?

23:50.080 --> 23:51.255
Um...

23:51.952 --> 23:54.433
And they're still fighting.

23:56.957 --> 24:00.656
So who messed up, then,
in this process?

24:01.091 --> 24:05.356
[EXHALES] I wish I knew.
You know? [CHUCKLES]

24:05.400 --> 24:06.793
I don't know.

24:06.836 --> 24:09.883
I don't feel
like I understand that.

24:15.671 --> 24:18.457
[SCIENCE LECTURE
PLAYS AT HIGH SPEED]

24:25.507 --> 24:27.378
Do you think
that's part of the reason

24:27.422 --> 24:30.512
why people can't come around
to you,

24:30.817 --> 24:32.732
is because they feel

24:32.775 --> 24:35.909
like you're not admitting
to the things that you did?

24:35.952 --> 24:37.127
Probably.

24:37.171 --> 24:38.651
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

24:39.303 --> 24:41.262
How's that make you feel?

24:42.611 --> 24:47.442
I mean, it makes it hard
to stand up for the truth,

24:47.486 --> 24:49.488
but I would never be able

24:49.531 --> 24:52.099
to live with myself
if I didn't.

24:54.449 --> 24:57.496
[SCIENCE LECTURE
PLAYS AT HIGH SPEED]

24:59.672 --> 25:01.500
What do you feel like
is the hardest thing

25:01.543 --> 25:04.067
for you to do, emotionally?

25:06.026 --> 25:07.636
What do you mean?

25:07.680 --> 25:11.510
Like, when it comes
to emotions,

25:11.553 --> 25:13.642
what's the hardest thing
for you?

25:16.602 --> 25:19.518
I mean, it took me
a really long time to

25:20.910 --> 25:24.566
to be okay with, um...

25:26.525 --> 25:28.439
with letting
someone see me cry.

25:31.747 --> 25:34.533
Um, but...

25:39.276 --> 25:41.235
[SOFTLY] I don't know
if I'm strong enough.

25:49.548 --> 25:50.810
BILLY: At a high level,

25:50.853 --> 25:52.376
The New York Times
is unwilling to change

25:52.420 --> 25:54.857
the "I created a character,"

25:54.901 --> 25:56.076
even though we told them,

25:56.119 --> 25:57.294
like, look,
we've got all these tapes.

25:57.338 --> 25:58.731
It wasn't said anywhere.

25:59.253 --> 26:00.559
And they feel strongly

26:00.602 --> 26:02.343
that she took sufficient notes
to cover that,

26:02.386 --> 26:03.518
but they don't have--

26:03.562 --> 26:04.824
like, they haven't
come back saying,

26:04.867 --> 26:06.173
well here's the place
that you said it,

26:06.216 --> 26:09.480
or, this, like,
we have any recording of this.

26:09.524 --> 26:11.831
They just say that
they stand by their reporting.

26:12.135 --> 26:15.095
Well,
let Liz describe it to you.

26:15.138 --> 26:17.184
Do they have proof
you said it?

26:17.227 --> 26:18.446
I don't know.

26:18.489 --> 26:20.317
I... I know that we--

26:20.361 --> 26:22.276
as she would speak with us,

26:22.319 --> 26:23.843
we would record
the conversations, too,

26:23.886 --> 26:25.627
'cause that's what
our comms person said to do.

26:25.671 --> 26:26.846
NATHAN: Yeah, yeah.
I heard that.

26:26.889 --> 26:28.674
But we don't have
any recording

26:28.717 --> 26:30.589
of me saying,
"I created a character."

26:30.632 --> 26:32.025
You should find out
if there's a recording.

26:32.068 --> 26:33.504
-No recording. We've asked--
-Don't you think?

26:33.548 --> 26:34.984
Let's go. Lizzie, come on.

26:35.028 --> 26:36.464
-I got-- Okay.
-Okay. Sorry.

26:36.507 --> 26:37.944
-To be continued. Yeah.
-Okay. To be continued.

26:37.987 --> 26:39.293
'Cause I do-- Okay.

26:39.336 --> 26:40.599
-Yeah, let's talk about it.
-I wanna ask,

26:40.642 --> 26:41.861
just 'cause the thing that--

26:41.904 --> 26:43.079
I know it's such
a small thing,

26:43.123 --> 26:44.167
The New York Timesthing,

26:44.211 --> 26:46.126
but the reason
I'm so interested is

26:46.169 --> 26:48.955
because
if you didn't say this,

26:48.998 --> 26:51.610
it really shows
the narrative...

26:51.653 --> 26:53.046
-[BOY CRYING]
-...of people trying to...

26:53.089 --> 26:55.222
-Make me into that.
-Turn you into that.

26:55.265 --> 26:57.485
But if you did say it,

26:57.528 --> 26:59.139
it speaks to the fact that

26:59.182 --> 27:00.923
maybe you don't
recall some things

27:00.967 --> 27:02.272
that you've said or done.

27:02.316 --> 27:03.926
Yeah.

27:03.970 --> 27:07.843
So the answer to this question
feels very relevant.

27:07.887 --> 27:08.975
-For sure.
-Yeah.

27:09.018 --> 27:10.629
My process was my memory,

27:10.672 --> 27:13.153
and then reviewing all
our tapes, and confirming...

27:13.196 --> 27:14.371
-Yeah.
-...and then following up

27:14.415 --> 27:15.416
based on that. You know?

27:15.459 --> 27:16.896
BILLY: Hey, Lizzie?

27:16.939 --> 27:19.246
Okay. Yeah. I know you have
to go, so, anyways.

27:19.289 --> 27:20.508
-Yeah. To be continued.
-[CHUCKLES] Okay. Great.

27:20.551 --> 27:21.640
-All right.
-Thanks, Liz.

27:21.683 --> 27:22.858
[BOY PROTESTING]

27:22.902 --> 27:24.686
ELIZABETH: Hey, sweetie.
Hey, sweetie.

27:25.556 --> 27:26.819
BOY: Mama?

27:26.862 --> 27:28.037
ELIZABETH: Yeah, I guess
they were filming

27:28.081 --> 27:29.560
the screen as I was writing.

27:29.604 --> 27:31.998
So then I was like,
trying to hide the sections

27:32.041 --> 27:33.869
that I'm not sure
if I wanna include or not.

27:33.913 --> 27:36.524
BILLY: Then just tell 'em
not to film the screen.

27:36.567 --> 27:38.134
ELIZABETH: Yeah.

27:38.178 --> 27:40.180
BILLY: Lizzie, you've got to
fucking stand up for yourself.

27:40.223 --> 27:41.268
Okay?

27:42.486 --> 27:44.010
ELIZABETH: Well, this whole
thing is about like,

27:44.053 --> 27:45.576
whether I'm like, an honest
and transparent person.

27:45.620 --> 27:47.143
So, like--

27:47.187 --> 27:48.710
BILLY: There's a difference
between being transparent

27:48.754 --> 27:51.104
and, like,
not feeling comfortable

27:51.147 --> 27:52.322
sharing something
that you don't know--

27:52.366 --> 27:53.410
[MICROPHONE SCRAPING]

28:03.769 --> 28:04.900
I mean, just to be clear,

28:04.944 --> 28:07.294
this person has
no deep experience.

28:07.337 --> 28:09.862
This is a student
with a passion

28:09.905 --> 28:11.864
for trying
to highlight problems

28:11.907 --> 28:13.909
that exist
within the legal system,

28:13.953 --> 28:15.998
who is interested in

28:16.042 --> 28:18.522
dedicating herself
and her life

28:18.566 --> 28:20.002
to that continued pursuit.

28:21.525 --> 28:23.005
I'm telling you,

28:23.049 --> 28:24.920
you guys just kill
the Wi-Fi every time.

28:24.964 --> 28:26.792
I mean,
when I was on the hearing,

28:26.835 --> 28:28.141
it was like, dropping.

28:28.184 --> 28:29.969
Just what makes us,

28:30.012 --> 28:31.927
our ability to do business
like, untenable.

28:31.971 --> 28:33.842
I don't think it has anything
to do with our equipment.

28:33.886 --> 28:35.235
It 100% does.

28:35.278 --> 28:37.367
Because like, at night,
I've got zero problems.

28:37.411 --> 28:39.021
It's only
when you guys are here.

28:39.065 --> 28:40.153
Something's interfering

28:40.196 --> 28:42.155
'cause like,
even when I tried to do

28:42.198 --> 28:44.723
that hearing the other day,
it dropped like, 10 times.

28:44.766 --> 28:45.985
-Really?
-Yeah.

28:46.028 --> 28:48.291
I feel like we're
on different frequencies.

28:48.335 --> 28:50.859
Right? It's not--

28:51.512 --> 28:53.470
I think it's the monitors.

28:53.514 --> 28:54.863
I'm unable
to connect to this Wi-Fi.

28:54.907 --> 28:56.822
I'm unable
to connect to our Wi-Fi.

28:56.865 --> 28:58.127
Would it be the monitors?

28:58.171 --> 28:59.738
-I think it's this--
-There we go.

29:00.477 --> 29:02.001
-Hi.
-Hi.

29:02.044 --> 29:03.437
Can you guys hear me?

29:03.480 --> 29:04.960
-Yeah. Sorry.
-We were on mute.

29:05.004 --> 29:07.093
We were having
serious Wi-Fi issues.

29:07.658 --> 29:09.748
So nice to meet you.

29:09.791 --> 29:11.271
STUDENT: So nice to meet you.

29:11.314 --> 29:12.533
Would you know,
was the fact that

29:12.576 --> 29:14.230
he was involved
in similar cases,

29:14.274 --> 29:16.537
was that disclosed at trial?

29:16.580 --> 29:18.495
BILLY: They were able
to get into it

29:18.539 --> 29:19.845
at like, the highest level,

29:19.888 --> 29:21.803
-but not really, no.
-Okay.

29:21.847 --> 29:24.284
The entire government's case
was predicated on this idea

29:24.327 --> 29:26.939
that the technology
didn't work. Right?

29:26.982 --> 29:28.592
But they had
no evidence to that.

29:28.636 --> 29:30.377
-So they had three patients...
-Right.

29:30.420 --> 29:31.639
...all of which Liz
was acquitted of.

29:31.682 --> 29:33.293
-Like, each of those counts.
-Yes.

29:33.336 --> 29:34.990
And then,
they had nothing else,

29:35.034 --> 29:37.427
so they brought
in this expert witness

29:37.471 --> 29:38.733
and had him say
whatever the fuck

29:38.777 --> 29:40.082
they wanted him to say.

29:40.126 --> 29:41.562
-Right. Right.
-Like,

29:41.605 --> 29:43.303
I mean, it's just procedurally
totally incorrect.

29:43.346 --> 29:45.435
I mean, you know,
some of the questions

29:45.479 --> 29:48.003
that I would have
for you guys,

29:48.047 --> 29:49.352
I guess, would be--

29:49.396 --> 29:51.746
[GARBLED STATIC]

29:57.317 --> 29:58.361
I'm...

29:59.536 --> 30:02.670
[GARBLED STATIC CONTINUES]

30:03.366 --> 30:05.238
I feel like she was about to
make an important point.

30:06.456 --> 30:07.718
ELIZABETH: Can call her.

30:10.112 --> 30:11.548
BILLY: Every single time
that you guys are here,

30:11.592 --> 30:13.202
the Wi-Fi does not work.

30:13.246 --> 30:14.595
NATHAN: And so
you're thinking

30:14.638 --> 30:16.815
of working with her
on your case?

30:16.858 --> 30:20.427
Um, you know,
the truth is like,

30:20.470 --> 30:23.865
I don't have the money
to pay her to do that.

30:23.909 --> 30:25.562
So the question would be like,

30:25.606 --> 30:28.652
would there be a role for her

30:28.696 --> 30:30.872
in, like, the telling of
our story? I don't know.

30:30.916 --> 30:33.135
But she could
help give context

30:33.179 --> 30:35.572
as to where there are strings
to be pulled.

30:35.616 --> 30:37.661
You know,
it's this weird thing because

30:37.966 --> 30:40.099
I've seen a lot of places

30:40.142 --> 30:41.883
where I think
there's just like,

30:41.927 --> 30:43.754
fundamental wrongs.

30:43.798 --> 30:46.888
And, you know, I hope that
you guys can look into it.

30:46.932 --> 30:49.195
Because I think
it will yield results

30:49.238 --> 30:51.937
that will really
surprise everyone.

31:12.914 --> 31:14.873
But it takes time and energy
and effort

31:14.916 --> 31:19.051
and like, passion and somebody
to care about it. So.

31:22.881 --> 31:24.708
So maybe you have it, Nathan,

31:25.579 --> 31:27.189
the ability
to think independently

31:27.233 --> 31:28.930
and actually like,
look into facts.

31:28.974 --> 31:30.105
'Cause the rest of the world

31:30.149 --> 31:31.890
seems totally
fucking incapable of it.

31:36.503 --> 31:37.896
Any thoughts?

31:37.939 --> 31:39.723
-Hm?
-Any thoughts?

31:40.463 --> 31:42.204
I was just listening.

31:47.862 --> 31:48.950
Okay.

31:57.393 --> 31:59.395
[BABY COOING]

32:03.747 --> 32:06.620
[ELIZABETH HUMS]

32:07.926 --> 32:09.188
I still have
this weird feeling

32:09.231 --> 32:11.886
that there's some things that

32:12.278 --> 32:15.498
I'm not seeing fully
about you guys.

32:17.283 --> 32:20.851
So I know you guys invited me
to stay here with you

32:20.895 --> 32:24.290
and I declined the offer.

32:24.333 --> 32:25.900
But I've been thinking maybe

32:26.945 --> 32:29.208
if I do take you up
on your offer

32:29.251 --> 32:30.644
and to really be here--

32:30.687 --> 32:33.038
You are welcome to be here.

32:34.387 --> 32:38.565
Does any part of you worry
about me being here 24-7?

32:38.608 --> 32:41.046
I don't think so.

32:41.829 --> 32:43.874
That's what you asked for.

32:44.745 --> 32:47.226
Is to see everything, so,

32:47.966 --> 32:51.665
this is... this is everything.

32:51.708 --> 32:52.883
You know?

32:53.232 --> 32:55.408
But what if I see something
that makes you look bad,

32:55.451 --> 32:56.757
that makes you look guilty?

32:56.800 --> 32:58.019
Really guilty?

32:58.063 --> 32:59.978
I'm not guilty.

33:00.021 --> 33:01.980
But what if I see something
that makes you look that way?

33:02.023 --> 33:04.460
Then you'll ask questions
about it.

33:05.809 --> 33:07.942
You really feel
you did nothing wrong?

33:07.986 --> 33:09.770
I did not.

33:13.600 --> 33:16.081
You never tried
to deceive anyone?

33:16.124 --> 33:17.996
I've never tried
to deceive anyone.

33:18.039 --> 33:19.562
Ever in your life?

33:19.606 --> 33:21.434
No.

33:22.130 --> 33:23.653
Tell me this.

33:24.306 --> 33:27.353
Have you ever lied
in your life?

33:32.314 --> 33:34.403
I don't think so.

33:34.447 --> 33:36.666
-I mean, I don't-- I can't--
-Liz, what?

33:36.710 --> 33:39.539
You've never lied
in your life?

33:39.582 --> 33:42.585
You've never
said something untrue?

33:42.629 --> 33:45.501
-Not intentionally.
-A white lie?

33:49.244 --> 33:51.159
I mean, I don't know,
when I was a kid.

33:51.203 --> 33:52.465
You know? Or, like--

33:52.508 --> 33:55.250
But I've always tried
to say what I believe.

33:55.294 --> 33:56.991
Like, that's how
I've built my life.

33:57.035 --> 34:00.995
That's why this is
so important to me.

34:02.605 --> 34:04.825
What's your definition
of a lie?

34:05.695 --> 34:09.264
Intentionally saying something
you believe to be untrue.

34:12.920 --> 34:15.792
Like, let's say, you know,
you didn't wanna go to a party

34:15.836 --> 34:20.145
like a friend's, or like,
an acquaintance's party.

34:20.188 --> 34:23.017
And you're just like,
oh, I'm like,

34:23.061 --> 34:24.714
not feeling great.
I don't-- I don't--

34:24.758 --> 34:26.368
You've never done
something like that?

34:26.412 --> 34:28.022
Or if you don't like someone,
you kinda--

34:28.066 --> 34:29.241
I'm sure-- I mean,

34:29.284 --> 34:31.765
I was thinking
about lies as like,

34:31.808 --> 34:34.550
the kind of things
that I've been accused of.

34:35.812 --> 34:36.987
But--

34:37.031 --> 34:39.425
Well you've done lies
like that?

34:39.468 --> 34:41.296
Telling a friend I feel sick
when I don't?

34:41.340 --> 34:42.950
-Yeah.
-I mean--

34:42.993 --> 34:44.212
-Mama?
-Yeah.

34:44.256 --> 34:45.431
Mama's jacket.

34:45.474 --> 34:47.955
That's Mommy's jacket.
Thank you.

34:47.998 --> 34:49.957
Is that Mommy's jacket
or Daddy's jacket?

34:50.000 --> 34:51.741
-Mama's.
-Mama's jacket?

34:51.785 --> 34:53.308
I'm gonna take him
down to the beach.

34:53.352 --> 34:54.570
Okay.

35:04.928 --> 35:06.016
[CELLPHONE CHIMES]

35:58.852 --> 36:00.288
[KNOCKS]

36:01.898 --> 36:04.162
"This adhesion is essential
for cell survival."

36:04.205 --> 36:06.729
"If cells become detached
from their microenvironment,"

36:06.773 --> 36:07.817
"they will die
through a process

36:07.861 --> 36:09.341
"known as apoptosis."

36:09.384 --> 36:10.733
"This phenomenon,

36:10.777 --> 36:12.996
"which is called adhesion
dependency of survival."

36:13.040 --> 36:16.870
Hey, is that thing
on the bed all there is,

36:16.913 --> 36:18.959
or is there a mattress, too?

36:19.481 --> 36:20.787
ELIZABETH: Are you
talking about, for in there?

36:20.830 --> 36:22.136
For the guest house. Yeah.

36:22.180 --> 36:24.051
-Uh...
-Yeah, we've got everything

36:24.094 --> 36:25.400
-in the storage containers.
-We have mattresses.

36:25.444 --> 36:26.880
We have to get it
out of the U-Haul.

36:26.923 --> 36:28.360
Oh, okay, okay.

36:28.403 --> 36:30.057
Just wasn't sure
if it was only the box.

36:30.100 --> 36:32.364
Yeah, we like sleeping
on just bed frames here.

36:32.407 --> 36:33.495
-Yeah.
-Okay.

36:54.037 --> 36:55.430
NATHAN: Doesn't that mess
with your head, though,

36:55.474 --> 36:58.172
when money's involved,
like that much money?

36:58.216 --> 37:01.480
No. It's not--
That's not what motivates me.

37:01.523 --> 37:02.437
[NATHAN SCOFFS]

37:03.960 --> 37:05.223
NATHAN: What about
when the company

37:05.266 --> 37:07.399
started to be worth
so much money?

37:07.442 --> 37:08.443
Mm.

37:09.314 --> 37:11.490
I'd never once sold a share.

37:12.491 --> 37:15.885
It was never
what this was about.

37:25.634 --> 37:30.248
Can I play you a piece
of audio that you said--

37:30.813 --> 37:32.902
It's a clip
from this interview you did

37:32.946 --> 37:37.342
with this journalist
for Fortune,um...

37:37.820 --> 37:39.039
-Parloff, I think...
-Okay.

37:39.082 --> 37:40.432
...was his name.

37:40.475 --> 37:41.737
ROGER PARLOFF: [OVER RECORDING]
Is it more

37:41.781 --> 37:42.999
in the nature
of a trade secret

37:43.043 --> 37:44.740
that you don't want them
to know

37:44.784 --> 37:46.612
you're at the analyzer phase
yet?

37:46.655 --> 37:48.091
ELIZABETH: That's right.
It's more

37:48.135 --> 37:49.528
in the context
of the trade secret.

37:49.571 --> 37:54.663
We're still filing
a lot of patents around it.

37:54.707 --> 37:56.665
The fact that
we have a single device

37:56.709 --> 37:58.798
that can perform any test

37:58.841 --> 38:01.017
-is a big deal.
-PARLOFF: Mm-hm.

38:01.061 --> 38:04.194
And so I mean, if we think
about this in terms of--

38:04.238 --> 38:05.674
So you say there,

38:06.153 --> 38:08.198
"The fact that
we have a single device

38:08.242 --> 38:11.158
"that can perform any test
is a big deal."

38:11.680 --> 38:15.031
But, from what I know,
you couldn't do

38:15.075 --> 38:17.817
every test on a single device.
That's the whole problem.

38:17.860 --> 38:20.298
That's why you're going
to prison. Right?

38:21.168 --> 38:22.343
No.

38:22.387 --> 38:24.780
That was our invention.
And in the case,

38:24.824 --> 38:28.784
I talked about the fact that
we had invented a device

38:28.828 --> 38:31.265
that could do any test.

38:32.527 --> 38:33.789
But if you had
a single device

38:33.833 --> 38:35.922
that could run
any test then...

38:36.836 --> 38:38.490
wasn't that the whole thing?

38:40.840 --> 38:43.886
I don't know what you mean by
"the whole thing,"

38:43.930 --> 38:46.802
but what you said
is not the case,

38:46.846 --> 38:49.370
and hopefully
I can explain that.

38:49.414 --> 38:50.980
-What I said's not the case?
-Mm-hm.

38:51.024 --> 38:52.547
What do you mean?

38:52.591 --> 38:54.984
That those statements
were untrue.

38:55.724 --> 38:58.771
-That's a true statement?
-Yes.

38:58.814 --> 39:01.600
That you have a single device
that can perform any test?

39:01.643 --> 39:04.080
We did.
That's what we invented.

39:36.504 --> 39:40.290
So this is the famous
scam device?

39:40.595 --> 39:42.249
It is not a scam device.

39:42.989 --> 39:46.384
But, that's what people think.

39:46.993 --> 39:48.168
Right?

39:49.169 --> 39:50.736
I don't know
what people think.

39:50.779 --> 39:53.521
What I know
is what we invented.

39:53.565 --> 39:55.567
And, um...

39:58.178 --> 40:01.181
And to me
the technology's amazing.

40:01.224 --> 40:04.053
So this works, you're saying?

40:04.097 --> 40:09.276
It did. Right now these are
just components of a system.

40:09.319 --> 40:10.495
It opens itself.

40:11.321 --> 40:13.019
Did you invent this?

40:13.759 --> 40:14.760
ELIZABETH: Um...

40:16.109 --> 40:18.285
How it opens like that?

40:18.328 --> 40:21.680
Our team did. I don't remember
who the inventor was on that.

40:21.723 --> 40:23.464
That wasn't your idea?

40:24.160 --> 40:26.293
I don't-- I don't remember.

40:29.296 --> 40:34.344
Our technologies worked
by allowing the cartridge,

40:34.388 --> 40:38.087
when it was inserted
into a device,

40:38.131 --> 40:40.394
to call the cloud

40:40.786 --> 40:43.745
and send protocols
from the cloud

40:43.789 --> 40:45.399
down to the device,

40:46.139 --> 40:48.881
telling you what to do
with the cartridge

40:48.924 --> 40:51.144
that had just been put in.

40:51.884 --> 40:53.842
And we developed that

40:53.886 --> 40:57.411
for all these different
methods of chemistry

40:57.455 --> 40:59.195
that we were running, tests.

40:59.979 --> 41:03.069
So in that way,
you can run any test

41:03.112 --> 41:04.940
because it's based on

41:04.984 --> 41:09.118
the cloud sending information
to a device,

41:09.162 --> 41:10.511
and you can preload

41:10.555 --> 41:13.340
any different type
of chemistry

41:13.383 --> 41:14.863
into the cartridge.

41:14.907 --> 41:16.517
BILLY: Does that make sense?

41:17.300 --> 41:20.216
So the device is different
than the chemistry.

41:20.260 --> 41:21.479
ELIZABETH: Exactly.

41:22.218 --> 41:23.916
The device is capable...

41:23.959 --> 41:26.396
-ELIZABETH: Yeah.
-...of running any test.

41:26.440 --> 41:27.876
ELIZABETH: Yes.

41:27.920 --> 41:29.791
But, currently,

41:31.053 --> 41:35.014
the device cannot run
every test.

41:35.057 --> 41:38.887
Well, you have to develop
the small sample chemistries.

41:38.931 --> 41:41.455
-For each test.
-Yeah. Validate them.

41:41.499 --> 41:43.675
And, for certain tests,
I guess,

41:43.718 --> 41:47.940
that you hadn't
done successfully on it,

41:47.983 --> 41:49.463
it's theoretical.

41:49.507 --> 41:52.205
Yeah. Because this
is a platform

41:52.248 --> 41:54.555
that can run those things.

41:55.338 --> 41:58.777
What you're referring to
is a future-based...

41:59.299 --> 42:02.911
The device can--
Is capable of running any--

42:02.955 --> 42:04.739
It's the word, I guess, "can".

42:04.783 --> 42:06.524
What I'm talking about here is
what we invented.

42:06.567 --> 42:08.351
-NATHAN: What you invented.
-Yeah.

42:09.048 --> 42:13.531
So when you said
the device can run any test,

42:13.574 --> 42:15.707
what you meant was,

42:15.750 --> 42:20.886
the device is capable
of running any test.

42:20.929 --> 42:22.670
What I'm talking about here

42:22.714 --> 42:26.631
is that the device is capable
of running any test, yes.

42:26.674 --> 42:30.156
Assuming additional chemistry
is developed.

42:30.199 --> 42:32.158
Assuming you have
the chemistries

42:32.201 --> 42:34.203
to do those things.

42:34.247 --> 42:37.685
But, do you understand that

42:38.425 --> 42:41.950
if someone heard,
"The device can run any test",

42:41.994 --> 42:45.040
how they could
interpret that as,

42:45.084 --> 42:50.742
"Currently, this device
is running any test"?

42:51.786 --> 42:54.572
I think
we provided information

42:54.615 --> 42:56.399
about the tests

42:56.443 --> 42:59.751
that we had developed

42:59.794 --> 43:01.317
and were developing and--

43:01.361 --> 43:05.974
'Cause people were saying,
"Oh, it can only run 12 tests"

43:06.018 --> 43:08.803
on this thing.

43:10.718 --> 43:12.154
-What I'm-- what I'm--
-I think the problem is,

43:12.198 --> 43:13.547
the thing that you're asking,
Nathan, is, like,

43:13.591 --> 43:15.593
what the general public
understood

43:15.636 --> 43:16.898
in Liz saying this thing.

43:16.942 --> 43:18.508
Like, what--

43:18.552 --> 43:20.728
Like, you're communicating
this thing

43:20.772 --> 43:22.687
and everyone got
a different picture

43:22.730 --> 43:24.732
than what you meant.

43:27.779 --> 43:29.432
I was-- I mean,

43:29.476 --> 43:31.086
I was just trying
to communicate

43:31.130 --> 43:33.175
the best I knew how. You know?

43:33.219 --> 43:35.482
And you were trying your best
to be clear?

43:35.525 --> 43:37.049
Of course.

43:44.056 --> 43:48.234
'Cause, when you say this
and describe this, I'm like,

43:49.061 --> 43:52.673
Well, wait a sec. I guess,
yeah, the device can--

43:53.152 --> 43:57.243
Like, that's right,
what you're saying.

43:57.939 --> 44:02.857
But, I'm also, like, "Well,
why choose those words?"

44:02.901 --> 44:07.645
Like, why not just, like,

44:07.688 --> 44:11.518
add one word
that would make it clear?

44:11.561 --> 44:13.041
But...

44:14.042 --> 44:18.003
I'm a bad communicator.
I don't know a lot of words.

44:19.308 --> 44:21.267
Is that what you're like?

44:23.312 --> 44:24.662
I...

44:27.665 --> 44:31.712
I feel like I know
I was trying to--

44:32.495 --> 44:35.542
to fully communicate
what we'd created,

44:35.585 --> 44:38.806
and I feel like now

44:38.850 --> 44:41.113
I'm living
in a parallel universe.

44:46.466 --> 44:48.555
I don't-- Yeah, I don't--

44:52.515 --> 44:54.692
I know what you're feeling.

44:56.171 --> 44:58.173
No, you don't.

45:03.439 --> 45:05.659
[CELL PHONE VIBRATES]

45:05.703 --> 45:06.965
Oh, I gotta quickly take this.

45:07.008 --> 45:08.140
-Oh.
-Legal call.

45:08.183 --> 45:09.663
NATHAN: Oh, okay.

45:09.707 --> 45:11.143
ELIZABETH: Would you mind
cutting the mic for a second?

45:11.186 --> 45:12.710
-Oh, sure. Yeah.
-ELIZABETH: Thank you.

45:14.624 --> 45:15.800
Hello?

45:35.645 --> 45:39.562
You've never been suspicious
that she's ever lied to you?

45:39.606 --> 45:41.129
No, even when
it's uncomfortable.

45:41.173 --> 45:42.304
Never?

45:42.348 --> 45:43.784
No. Never.

45:43.828 --> 45:45.917
I know it seems weird, but--

45:45.960 --> 45:47.527
And is, like, you can shower

45:47.570 --> 45:50.095
whenever you want,
or at certain times?

45:50.138 --> 45:55.056
BILLY: If I tell somebody
I'm busy, is that a lie?

45:55.100 --> 45:56.710
-NATHAN: If you're not?
-Yeah.

45:56.754 --> 45:57.972
-Yes.
-Okay.

45:58.016 --> 45:59.365
-So I'll say that.
-Yeah.

45:59.408 --> 46:00.801
And Liz will be like, yeah,
Billy's available.

46:00.845 --> 46:02.368
He's right here.

46:02.411 --> 46:04.849
-Right. So you lie.
-So I get fucked.

46:04.892 --> 46:06.938
-You lie, and she doesn't.
-Yes. Yes, absolutely.

46:06.981 --> 46:08.417
Not that I've known her to.

46:08.461 --> 46:10.637
So you have to wake up
at 3:30?

46:10.680 --> 46:12.639
And I know
it seems totally improbable.

46:12.682 --> 46:14.467
I understand that.
It's weird.

46:14.510 --> 46:16.556
But she's really weird.

46:16.861 --> 46:18.514
She's just not normal.

46:18.558 --> 46:20.908
[MUSIC PLAYING]

46:20.952 --> 46:24.564
But the fact that
she's most famous for lying...

46:24.607 --> 46:25.826
For that thing, yeah.

46:25.870 --> 46:27.436
The thing she's never done,

46:28.655 --> 46:30.396
you believe that?

46:30.439 --> 46:32.267
You actually believe
since high school,

46:32.311 --> 46:34.748
she's never told a single lie?

46:34.792 --> 46:37.359
What is an invasive
strip search?

46:37.403 --> 46:39.535
She's just gotten
really fucked, man.

46:39.579 --> 46:41.799
But what's your theory
on that? It's random?

46:41.842 --> 46:43.888
No, it's not random.
It's, like, purposeful.

46:43.931 --> 46:47.065
But where did it start?

46:47.108 --> 46:49.589
'Cause it didn't start
with the investors.

46:49.632 --> 46:51.634
-No.
-It started with a journalist.

46:51.678 --> 46:53.245
-Yeah. So...
-I think you should

46:53.288 --> 46:54.855
look into that.

46:54.899 --> 46:58.032
But why? He just picked
a random person and--

46:58.076 --> 47:00.774
No. He was put onto the story
by her competition.

47:00.818 --> 47:03.472
Can you see visitors
that first weekend?

47:03.516 --> 47:05.474
Is there evidence
that that happened?

47:05.518 --> 47:07.912
I think you--
I think that there is...

47:07.955 --> 47:09.696
Um, there's a lot to find.

47:09.739 --> 47:12.786
Why're you being so weird
and cryptic with your words?

47:12.830 --> 47:14.962
Say what you're--
What's there.

47:15.006 --> 47:17.399
[CHOKED UP]
Okay. All right. Thank you.

47:17.704 --> 47:19.401
NATHAN: You--
You want this to--

47:19.445 --> 47:23.275
BILLY: What I'm telling you
is, is--

47:25.407 --> 47:27.845
-Liz, are you okay?
-ELIZABETH: [SNIFFLES] Yeah.

47:29.281 --> 47:30.282
BILLY: Hey.

47:34.503 --> 47:35.548
[SPEAKS INDISTINCTLY]

47:37.767 --> 47:39.508
[SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

48:06.840 --> 48:10.322
Do you know who
Abbott and Costello are?

48:10.975 --> 48:12.150
-No.
-Not really.

48:12.193 --> 48:13.281
No.

48:15.327 --> 48:18.852
Have you heard of the comedy
routine, "Who's On First?"

48:18.896 --> 48:19.853
Mm-mm.

48:19.897 --> 48:21.072
-You haven't?
-Mm-mm.

48:21.115 --> 48:23.465
Okay. Can I play this for you?

48:23.509 --> 48:25.554
-ELIZABETH: Mm-hm.
-Okay.

48:28.949 --> 48:30.690
Something we were
talking about the other day

48:30.733 --> 48:33.084
-made me think about this.
-Okay.

48:33.127 --> 48:34.520
BUD ABBOTT: Oh, sure.

48:34.563 --> 48:36.348
But you know they give
baseball players, nowadays,

48:36.391 --> 48:37.523
very peculiar names.

48:37.566 --> 48:39.394
I know, a lotta
funny names. Nicknames.

48:39.438 --> 48:40.613
