01:14:27.767 --> 01:14:29.203
I know. I know.

01:14:29.987 --> 01:14:32.772
I mean, hopefully I'm showing
that with my actions.

01:14:32.816 --> 01:14:34.470
I'm not just telling you
there's a truth.

01:14:34.513 --> 01:14:39.083
I'm living
by that belief in--

01:14:40.737 --> 01:14:43.000
But you're smart.
You're smart.

01:14:43.043 --> 01:14:44.567
Right?

01:14:45.132 --> 01:14:46.525
I don't--
I don't know about that.

01:14:46.569 --> 01:14:48.440
You're very smart.
Come on, right?

01:14:49.659 --> 01:14:50.921
I mean,
that's what we're fighting--

01:14:50.964 --> 01:14:52.226
That's why I'm not--
That's why I'm fighting.

01:14:52.270 --> 01:14:53.401
-Right?
-Okay.

01:14:53.445 --> 01:14:56.622
Otherwise, I would settle

01:14:56.666 --> 01:14:59.059
and have more time
with my babies,

01:14:59.103 --> 01:15:00.408
which is what I want more
than anything

01:15:00.452 --> 01:15:01.671
in the world. Right?

01:15:02.672 --> 01:15:03.847
Okay.

01:15:06.502 --> 01:15:07.938
All right.

01:15:08.765 --> 01:15:10.070
Okay, good chat.

01:15:21.691 --> 01:15:23.519
You're being real right now?

01:15:23.997 --> 01:15:25.695
I'm always being real.

01:15:30.874 --> 01:15:31.962
[BABY FUSSES]

01:15:32.005 --> 01:15:33.703
It's okay, buddy.

01:15:35.095 --> 01:15:36.140
It's okay.

01:16:01.339 --> 01:16:03.559
BILLY: I can't believe
we're moving to Texas.

01:16:08.128 --> 01:16:09.695
I know
it feels so wrong.

01:16:11.610 --> 01:16:13.743
ELIZABETH: I feel like this is
a really nice place.

01:16:15.222 --> 01:16:16.833
Look at how green it is.

01:16:18.269 --> 01:16:20.663
BILLY: Immediately,
it feels different.

01:16:22.621 --> 01:16:24.405
Looking forward
to looking at houses

01:16:24.449 --> 01:16:26.146
and finding a place
that's safe

01:16:26.190 --> 01:16:28.584
and secure
for Ivy and Wills.

01:16:30.237 --> 01:16:31.891
ELIZABETH:
Yeah, maybe we stay.

01:16:35.634 --> 01:16:38.724
BILLY: And we do have, like,
incredible friends here.

01:16:41.945 --> 01:16:43.163
[ELIZABETH SNIFFLING]

01:17:05.838 --> 01:17:09.625
I know this is your final
couple hours together.

01:17:09.668 --> 01:17:11.017
I don't know if you--

01:17:13.846 --> 01:17:16.849
We're all here,
but if you wanna have a moment

01:17:16.893 --> 01:17:18.459
I'm happy to step out

01:17:18.503 --> 01:17:21.419
and you guys
can have some time.

01:17:21.462 --> 01:17:23.116
But if not, that's okay, or--

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:24.640
Well we do need
to take a shower,

01:17:24.683 --> 01:17:26.729
'cause we didn't shower
this morning.

01:17:27.338 --> 01:17:28.818
I just--
To give you the option,

01:17:28.861 --> 01:17:30.515
'cause you're gonna be gone
for a very--

01:17:30.558 --> 01:17:32.256
You know,
it's a very long time.

01:17:32.299 --> 01:17:33.649
You won't be able to--

01:17:34.301 --> 01:17:36.956
to have sex,
so I'm happy to just leave.

01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:38.958
I can go in my car,

01:17:39.002 --> 01:17:41.047
if you want some time,
just the two of you.

01:17:41.091 --> 01:17:44.747
I'm happy to pull
the cameras out and go away.

01:17:48.751 --> 01:17:49.969
BILLY: I love you.

01:17:50.013 --> 01:17:51.057
[WEAKLY] I love you, too.

01:17:51.101 --> 01:17:52.711
Does he make you laugh?

01:17:52.755 --> 01:17:54.191
That's good.

01:17:57.107 --> 01:17:59.196
We could use
a few laughs today.

01:18:11.338 --> 01:18:12.688
[ELIZABETH SNIFFLES]

01:18:15.255 --> 01:18:16.343
[ELIZABETH SIGHS]

01:18:16.387 --> 01:18:18.650
All right, I'll just, uh--

01:18:20.696 --> 01:18:22.698
[ROCK MUSIC PLAYING
OVER STEREO]

01:18:39.540 --> 01:18:40.672
And there was this huge fight

01:18:40.716 --> 01:18:42.674
over Elizabeth's patent
during COVID,

01:18:42.718 --> 01:18:44.328
you know, when she was already

01:18:44.371 --> 01:18:45.938
not even associated
with the company,

01:18:45.982 --> 01:18:48.419
where people were arguing over
getting their hands on it.

01:18:48.462 --> 01:18:50.290
But none of that
is picked up on

01:18:50.334 --> 01:18:53.554
because it's all very frothy
right now.

01:18:53.598 --> 01:18:55.643
You know,
people are making a fortune,

01:18:55.687 --> 01:18:57.602
you know,
off of Elizabeth's story,

01:18:57.645 --> 01:18:59.125
the negative side
of her story,

01:18:59.169 --> 01:19:00.648
not the positive side.

01:19:00.692 --> 01:19:02.694
But you watch. I mean,
I'll be dead

01:19:02.738 --> 01:19:04.565
when this all happens,

01:19:04.609 --> 01:19:06.567
but you watch,
a few decades from now,

01:19:06.611 --> 01:19:07.830
-NATHAN: Okay.
-...the whole story

01:19:07.873 --> 01:19:10.093
is gonna be coming out
about my daughter.

01:19:10.136 --> 01:19:12.225
-Our daughter.
-Oh, hey.

01:19:13.139 --> 01:19:14.662
How was it?

01:19:15.881 --> 01:19:17.230
Good?

01:19:17.274 --> 01:19:19.667
-Yeah.
-Good.

01:19:19.711 --> 01:19:21.191
Okay, so we're gonna
print this,

01:19:21.234 --> 01:19:23.846
and then we're going
to head out.

01:19:23.889 --> 01:19:25.499
We're gonna grab food
along the way

01:19:25.543 --> 01:19:27.327
'cause we need
to get you something to eat,

01:19:27.371 --> 01:19:29.416
like maybe a smoothie.
Just something easy.

01:19:29.460 --> 01:19:31.549
-Yeah, let's get a smoothie.
-And then--

01:19:32.811 --> 01:19:35.596
-I'm gonna put these shoes on.
-Okay.

01:19:39.905 --> 01:19:44.301
Liz, I got you, like,
a little card and a thing.

01:19:44.344 --> 01:19:46.042
ELIZABETH: [SOFTLY]
You didn't have to do that.

01:19:47.130 --> 01:19:48.435
NATHAN: Um...

01:19:57.967 --> 01:19:59.403
-Thank you.
-NATHAN: No problem.

01:20:00.534 --> 01:20:03.276
This is kind of, like,
a bit of a gag gift

01:20:03.320 --> 01:20:05.713
but I didn't know your parents
would be here, or whatever.

01:20:05.757 --> 01:20:07.237
Well, I don't know if--

01:20:07.280 --> 01:20:08.716
Don't get offended--
It's just a, kind of,

01:20:08.760 --> 01:20:10.109
like, a joke.

01:20:10.893 --> 01:20:14.679
It was like, you know, like,
hair dye and glasses

01:20:14.722 --> 01:20:15.898
in case she wants
to, like, flee

01:20:15.941 --> 01:20:16.855
to another country,

01:20:16.899 --> 01:20:17.943
-last minute.
-[GRUNTS]

01:20:18.726 --> 01:20:20.163
Just a joke,

01:20:20.206 --> 01:20:22.600
sometimes I joke with her to--

01:20:22.643 --> 01:20:24.558
But, anyways,

01:20:24.602 --> 01:20:27.039
maybe not appropriate
for the moment.

01:20:30.260 --> 01:20:31.565
All right, should we do this?

01:20:31.609 --> 01:20:33.176
Okay, I sent you
the contact list.

01:20:33.219 --> 01:20:34.830
-Okay, great. Yeah. I got it.
-Thank you so much.

01:20:34.873 --> 01:20:36.048
So we got that.

01:20:36.092 --> 01:20:37.571
-I can put this in my bag.
-BILLY: Yeah.

01:20:37.615 --> 01:20:39.747
[CHOKED UP] I have milk
for Ivy in the fridge.

01:20:39.791 --> 01:20:40.836
BILLY: Okay.

01:20:42.402 --> 01:20:44.709
I'll bring it to her. Okay.
[SNIFFLES]

01:20:48.756 --> 01:20:50.802
I'm gonna bring it to her.

01:20:55.589 --> 01:20:56.721
BILLY: Thank you.

01:20:56.764 --> 01:20:58.114
ELIZABETH: My love,

01:20:58.157 --> 01:20:59.767
I'll keep my backpack
in the back seat with me.

01:20:59.811 --> 01:21:00.856
BILLY: Okay.

01:21:04.424 --> 01:21:05.512
-All right.
-ELIZABETH: We'll find ways

01:21:05.556 --> 01:21:07.558
-to talk.
-Okay.

01:21:09.342 --> 01:21:10.778
Okay.

01:21:11.605 --> 01:21:12.824
Be, uh...

01:21:15.609 --> 01:21:16.828
All right.

01:21:25.663 --> 01:21:27.012
You've got this.

01:21:30.363 --> 01:21:31.582
Okay.

01:21:32.975 --> 01:21:34.150
Okay.

01:21:41.592 --> 01:21:44.160
All right. Stay tough.

01:21:44.595 --> 01:21:46.162
Thank you.

01:23:25.739 --> 01:23:27.393
[IMPERCEPTIBLE]

01:23:53.550 --> 01:23:56.379
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

01:23:56.422 --> 01:23:59.121
[SHUTTER CLICKING RAPIDLY]

01:24:42.686 --> 01:24:44.209
BILLY: [OVER PHONE] Like,
we don't know exactly

01:24:44.253 --> 01:24:45.950
where there's going
to be roadblocks.

01:24:45.993 --> 01:24:47.299
But where there
are roadblocks,

01:24:47.343 --> 01:24:48.561
Liz will come up
with solutions

01:24:48.605 --> 01:24:49.693
to those roadblocks.

01:24:49.736 --> 01:24:51.173
And she'll come up
with new ideas.

01:24:51.216 --> 01:24:53.305
So, in a year,

01:24:53.349 --> 01:24:55.829
I think I'd like to have
a working prototype,

01:24:55.873 --> 01:24:57.875
a team of people assembled.

01:24:57.918 --> 01:25:01.357
And then I'd like to start
developing the first tests.

01:25:01.400 --> 01:25:03.576
NATHAN: Mm.
BILLY: Yeah.

01:25:03.620 --> 01:25:07.624
I tried getting ahold of
that New York Timesreporter

01:25:07.667 --> 01:25:09.843
to see if she had that quote.

01:25:10.366 --> 01:25:13.325
Yeah, well,
we recorded every interaction

01:25:13.369 --> 01:25:16.111
with this reporter,
every single one. Um...

01:25:17.373 --> 01:25:18.896
Well can you send them to me?

01:25:18.939 --> 01:25:20.767
No. I haven't checked

01:25:20.811 --> 01:25:23.030
the whole thing
for everything.

01:25:23.074 --> 01:25:25.032
All right. Well,

01:25:26.121 --> 01:25:28.253
so working prototype
within a year, you say.

01:25:28.297 --> 01:25:29.515
Yeah, that's the goal.

01:25:29.559 --> 01:25:31.952
NATHAN: Okay.
BILLY: That's the goal.

01:25:32.518 --> 01:25:36.957
NATHAN: I guess, give me
a call when it's done.

01:25:46.097 --> 01:25:49.013
[GUNSHOT]

01:26:01.460 --> 01:26:03.723
-MAN: Your company is here?
-Yeah.

01:26:03.767 --> 01:26:05.551
-Okay.
-Yeah.

01:26:07.031 --> 01:26:09.120
We use lasers.

01:26:34.101 --> 01:26:36.365
BILLY: There have been
developments over the course

01:26:36.408 --> 01:26:37.975
of the last handful of years,

01:26:38.018 --> 01:26:39.281
that the approach
that we are taking,

01:26:39.324 --> 01:26:41.152
and the way in which
we are solving the problem

01:26:41.196 --> 01:26:43.023
is uniquely possible today.

01:26:43.459 --> 01:26:46.853
Artificial intelligence
allows you to do that.

01:26:46.897 --> 01:26:48.855
And I think a lot of people
don't yet know

01:26:48.899 --> 01:26:50.422
that that's possible.

01:26:50.466 --> 01:26:51.771
-And the reason why we know...
-JOE: Yeah.

01:26:51.815 --> 01:26:53.817
...this is this is
because Joe, really, not me,

01:26:53.860 --> 01:26:55.862
has been at the forefront

01:26:55.906 --> 01:26:59.344
of the most innovative
technology in the world,

01:26:59.388 --> 01:27:00.867
for these subcomponents
and how they're used.

01:27:00.911 --> 01:27:02.826
We have a technology
where we can look

01:27:02.869 --> 01:27:05.176
at a specific molecule

01:27:05.220 --> 01:27:08.527
and start with extremely
high resolution,

01:27:08.571 --> 01:27:11.835
tell you what's different
about its fingerprint today

01:27:11.878 --> 01:27:13.489
than it was last week.

01:27:13.532 --> 01:27:16.492
I mean,
that changes everything

01:27:16.535 --> 01:27:18.058
in the medical universe.

01:27:18.102 --> 01:27:20.539
The purple boxes here
are lasers.

01:27:20.583 --> 01:27:21.845
Okay.

01:27:21.888 --> 01:27:23.455
So those lasers can be 10,000

01:27:23.499 --> 01:27:25.240
different wavelengths
of light, each.

01:27:25.283 --> 01:27:26.763
You send light
through a sample...

01:27:26.806 --> 01:27:28.068
NATHAN: Okay.
BILLY: ...blood or urine

01:27:28.112 --> 01:27:30.462
or saliva, that light changes
wavelength,

01:27:30.506 --> 01:27:32.943
depending on
what's inside of it.

01:27:32.986 --> 01:27:33.987
And once we catch that light,

01:27:34.031 --> 01:27:35.946
we then put it
into our computer

01:27:35.989 --> 01:27:37.600
to interpret the results.

01:27:37.643 --> 01:27:39.384
Is this the new device?

01:27:39.428 --> 01:27:43.388
These components
are part of the device.

01:27:43.432 --> 01:27:46.086
All of these will be shrunk
down into something

01:27:46.130 --> 01:27:48.263
about half the size
of a Kleenex box.

01:27:48.306 --> 01:27:49.699
So you can test
for disease with it,

01:27:49.742 --> 01:27:53.050
with accuracies around 96,
97 percent.

01:27:53.833 --> 01:27:57.620
Is it, like, harder to make
decisions together, now,

01:27:57.663 --> 01:27:59.361
you and Liz?

01:28:00.231 --> 01:28:02.146
I think there's just
so much trust that

01:28:03.843 --> 01:28:06.019
most things I don't really
consult her on.

01:28:06.063 --> 01:28:07.760
Like, I know what she'd want.

01:28:09.022 --> 01:28:10.328
I mean, I don't know
anybody else

01:28:10.372 --> 01:28:13.113
that has a relationship
as close as we have.

01:28:24.690 --> 01:28:26.431
[CHILDREN CHATTERING
INDISTINCTLY]

01:28:28.781 --> 01:28:30.870
BILLY: "I sink to the bottom"

01:28:30.914 --> 01:28:32.524
"beneath the crust
of the earth,

01:28:32.872 --> 01:28:34.221
[IN COMICAL VOICE]
"...into the magma flow."

01:28:34.265 --> 01:28:35.310
[BOY GIGGLES]

01:28:36.789 --> 01:28:39.226
BILLY: How long does it take
to take audio clips

01:28:39.270 --> 01:28:41.228
and then, like,
clone her voice?

01:28:41.838 --> 01:28:44.014
EXPERT: We can get
an initial version

01:28:44.057 --> 01:28:45.668
of it up and running

01:28:45.711 --> 01:28:48.105
in, like, 20 or 30 minutes.

01:28:48.148 --> 01:28:49.976
BILLY: Oh, really? Okay. Wow.

01:28:56.853 --> 01:28:58.333
It's so crazy

01:28:58.376 --> 01:29:02.162
because when this was taken,
she was--

01:29:02.206 --> 01:29:05.209
she had given birth like,
a month before.

01:29:05.252 --> 01:29:06.863
She looks so different now.

01:29:09.213 --> 01:29:10.606
Like, she looks
so much healthier.

01:29:12.129 --> 01:29:13.565
Now?

01:29:13.913 --> 01:29:15.132
Yeah.

01:29:15.175 --> 01:29:17.700
She doesn't look healthy here?

01:29:17.743 --> 01:29:19.266
No, it's not that
she doesn't look healthy--

01:29:19.310 --> 01:29:20.398
I don't mean healthy.

01:29:20.442 --> 01:29:21.704
That's the wrong way
to describe it.

01:29:21.747 --> 01:29:23.706
She looks so much more fit.

01:29:25.011 --> 01:29:27.187
Like she's got abs.

01:29:29.842 --> 01:29:32.062
Healthy is the wrong way
to describe it.

01:29:34.107 --> 01:29:35.631
JOE: That's what this
is all about,

01:29:35.674 --> 01:29:39.069
it's being able to look at
a person's, you know,

01:29:39.112 --> 01:29:43.073
body chemistry
at a molecular level.

01:29:43.116 --> 01:29:46.772
How do you feel about,
like, carrying on,

01:29:46.816 --> 01:29:50.646
or being a part of carrying on
the Theranos mission?

01:29:50.689 --> 01:29:52.648
JOE: If you're gonna
solve a problem,

01:29:52.691 --> 01:29:56.826
it happens
with multiple failures.

01:29:56.869 --> 01:29:59.306
Theranos is one failure
on a path,

01:29:59.350 --> 01:30:00.873
and there will be more.

01:30:00.917 --> 01:30:03.180
What do you feel
like they did wrong?

01:30:03.223 --> 01:30:05.008
Oh, they used
the wrong technology.

01:30:05.051 --> 01:30:06.488
And it was a step
along the way.

01:30:06.531 --> 01:30:07.924
And there're people
doing it right now.

01:30:07.967 --> 01:30:10.013
I appreciate
the line of questioning

01:30:10.056 --> 01:30:13.364
but I think that,
like, the truth is,

01:30:13.408 --> 01:30:15.801
Joe wasn't there, like--

01:30:15.845 --> 01:30:17.890
Well, I can ask--

01:30:17.934 --> 01:30:19.849
figure out
the question to ask.

01:30:20.458 --> 01:30:22.329
Is that okay?

01:30:24.375 --> 01:30:28.814
I look
at the Theranos endeavor

01:30:28.858 --> 01:30:32.122
as a wealth of information.

01:30:32.165 --> 01:30:33.384
Some things "what to do",

01:30:33.428 --> 01:30:35.081
and some things
"what not to do".

01:30:35.125 --> 01:30:37.562
And as far as how it imploded,
you know,

01:30:37.606 --> 01:30:38.824
the claims that they made.

01:30:38.868 --> 01:30:40.826
I mean, let me just put it
to you this way.

01:30:40.870 --> 01:30:44.351
Entrepreneurs,
even the best of them,

01:30:44.395 --> 01:30:45.701
you know, the Elon Musks,

01:30:45.744 --> 01:30:47.790
make claims that are not true

01:30:47.833 --> 01:30:49.661
at any given point in time.
Right?

01:30:49.705 --> 01:30:52.882
"We're gonna have
a self-driving car--"

01:30:52.925 --> 01:30:54.492
And people paid money for it.

01:30:54.536 --> 01:30:56.755
-So the point of--
-You're done. Right now.

01:30:56.799 --> 01:30:58.191
Wait. What's going on?

01:30:58.235 --> 01:31:00.106
You guys can turn off
the cameras, thank you.

01:31:00.150 --> 01:31:01.978
What's the issue?

01:31:02.021 --> 01:31:03.370
If you'd like
to talk about it,

01:31:03.414 --> 01:31:04.850
-turn off the cameras.
-I wanna know the issue.

01:31:04.894 --> 01:31:06.461
I'm asking you to turn off
the cameras so--

01:31:06.504 --> 01:31:08.419
Billy, you can't just walk in,
in the middle of an interview

01:31:08.463 --> 01:31:09.464
and say,
"turn off the cameras"

01:31:09.507 --> 01:31:10.465
-whenever you want.
-I didn't.

01:31:10.508 --> 01:31:11.422
You're not
the director of this.

01:31:11.466 --> 01:31:12.467
-I'm not the director.
-Yes.

01:31:12.510 --> 01:31:13.729
I'm not trying
to be the director.

01:31:13.772 --> 01:31:15.034
-NATHAN: Right.
-So I'm asking you

01:31:15.078 --> 01:31:16.079
to turn off the cameras.

01:31:16.122 --> 01:31:17.254
I'm not gonna engage
with you, so.

01:31:17.297 --> 01:31:18.560
Can you tell me?
I have no idea

01:31:18.603 --> 01:31:19.735
what you're talking
about right now.

01:31:19.778 --> 01:31:21.127
I can tell you when
the cameras are off,

01:31:21.171 --> 01:31:22.477
-but not talking about it.
-Okay. Sorry, Joe,

01:31:22.520 --> 01:31:23.956
-give me a second.
-As I've asked you now,

01:31:24.000 --> 01:31:25.349
-10 times to--
-Okay. Turn off the cameras.

01:31:37.317 --> 01:31:42.235
I submitted an application
to visit Liz in prison,

01:31:42.845 --> 01:31:43.976
with uh--

01:31:44.368 --> 01:31:47.023
to get the cameras in there.

01:31:47.066 --> 01:31:49.199
Oh, that's never gonna happen.

01:31:49.591 --> 01:31:50.940
Why?

01:31:52.724 --> 01:31:53.725
Uh...

01:31:57.207 --> 01:32:00.906
Because I know more about
what they are allowed to do.

01:32:00.950 --> 01:32:03.256
You think there's no chance
it's gonna happen?

01:32:03.779 --> 01:32:08.087
Chance is below negative.
It's not gonna happen.

01:32:08.131 --> 01:32:09.698
And I love how
we are repeating our story

01:32:09.741 --> 01:32:11.569
about the woman
who picked blueberries

01:32:11.613 --> 01:32:16.269
and changed the world
with her gesture of kindness

01:32:16.313 --> 01:32:18.620
